  "worked_solution": "The equilibrium (fixed) points of a system ${x}^{\u2032}(t)=f(x,y)$ are the points for which $f(x,y)=0.$ So, we get the following system of linear equations:\n\n$$\n{\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.25x)-xy=0 \\\\ -6y+2xy=0\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nFactoring the second equation, we get\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}-6y+2xy & =0 \\\\ y(-6+2x) & =0.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, $y=0$ or $x=3.$ We consider both cases separately.\n\n- If $y=0,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2x(1-0.25x)-x(0) & =0 \\\\ 2x(0.25x-1) & =0 \\\\ x & =0,\\,4.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, the first two equilibria are $(0,0)$ and $(4,0).$\n\n- If $x=3,$ substituting it into the first equation, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}2(3)(1-0.25(3))-(3)y & =0 \\\\ 1.5-3y & =0 \\\\ y & =0.5.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThus, another equilibrium is $(3,0.5).$\n\nTherefore, the equilibria of the model are\n\n$$\n(0,0),\\,(4,0),\\,(3,0.5).\n$$",
  "calculator_instructions": "",
